            <ArticleTitle>Chlamydia trachomatis Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome without salpingitis in female adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>996-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We encountered seven female adolescents with the Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome and no signs or symptoms of salpingitis. Six of the patients had cervical cultures positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. The Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome should be included in the differential diagnosis of right-sided abdominal pain in the sexually active female adolescent to avoid unnecessary diagnostic procedures and to reduce the prevalence of chlamydial infection and its complications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
